# Passwordless sudo for the Clawcius host agent's own service account.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-12, AND WHY THE PREVIOUS VERSION'S COMMENTS LIED
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# On 2026-08-12 this file was audited adversarially, by six independent lenses,
# and nine findings survived a two-of-three refutation round. Four of them were
# holes; the rest were sentences in these comments that were not true. Both
# kinds are fixed here, and the comments are the more important half: a security
# file that flatters itself is worse than one with no comments at all, because
# it is the thing the next reader trusts INSTEAD of reading the rules.
#
# **The mechanism behind every hole, because it is the thing to remember:**
#
#     sudo joins the arguments into ONE string and fnmatch()es it without
#     FNM_PATHNAME. So `*` matches spaces and `/`. A `*` in an ARGUMENT
#     position is therefore not "one argument", it is "any number of
#     arguments" — and what it absorbs is handed straight to a program that
#     re-parses it as flags.
#
# What that cost, all of it verified rather than reasoned about:
#
#   1. **`install -m 0644 -o root -g root * /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service`
#      was one command to unrestricted root.** The `*` sat between the pinned
#      flags and the destination, and GNU install applies repeated flags
#      last-wins. So
#          sudo install -m 0644 -o root -g root \
#            -t /etc/sudoers.d /home/npurcell/clawcius/x \
#            /etc/systemd/system/clawciusX.service
#      matched the rule — `-t` makes every remaining operand a SOURCE — and
#      wrote an attacker-authored file into /etc/sudoers.d, mode 0644 root:root,
#      which passes sudo's own secure-file check. Content:
#      `clawcius-ops ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL`. The trailing bogus source makes
#      install exit 1 AFTER the file has landed. Also confirmed: `-m 4755 -t
#      /tmp /bin/bash …` for a setuid-root shell (`install -m 0644 -m 0777`
#      really does produce 0777, coreutils 9.1), and
#      `install … /root/.ssh/id_ed25519 /etc/systemd/system/clawcius-leak.service`
#      to copy any root-only file somewhere world-readable.
#   2. **`rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service` deleted anything.** The
#      pattern anchors only the first and last token of the flattened string, so
#          rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawciusA.service \
#                /var/lib/clawcius-ops/journal.jsonl \
#                /etc/systemd/system/clawciusB.service
#      matched. That is the audit log, the circuit breaker and every armed
#      rollback deadline — the exact state this file claimed, twice, that no
#      rule here reaches.
#   3. **The "namespaced" destination climbed out.** `*` matches `/`, so
#      `/etc/systemd/system/clawcius/../ssh.service` matched both rules. The
#      comment below them said there was "no `..` that produces a path still
#      ending in a project-prefixed unit name" while flagging itself as "a
#      sentence worth distrusting". Distrust it: it was wrong, and it defeated
#      the whole "sshd and docker.service are DELIBERATELY ABSENT" argument —
#      those sections withheld systemctl verbs but not write access to the unit
#      files systemd reads.
#   4. **`journalctl *` was documented "read-only" and was not.** It permits
#      `--vacuum-time=1s`, `--vacuum-size=1`, `--rotate`, `--flush` and
#      `--relinquish-var`: erasing the host's log history, as root, in one
#      command. Together with (2) that is both halves of the audit trail, and
#      the design's entire stated fallback is "it is all in the log".
#   5. **`docker inspect *` was in a READ alias and dumped every container's
#      `Config.Env`** — i.e. the other agents' API keys — plus their mount
#      table. This file goes out of its way to disclose the far smaller
#      `systemctl show` leak and said nothing about this one, where the
#      equivalent excuse ("it grants nothing `cat` does not") is false: container
#      environment is not world-readable and this account is deliberately not in
#      the docker group, so the rule was the entire capability.
#
# The `""` added to `journalctl` in the previous change was a no-op, and saying
# so is part of the point of this header: `/usr/bin/journalctl *` was still on
# the same line, and "any arguments" ∪ "no arguments" is the same capability the
# bare entry granted. The `systemctl ""` half of that change was real and is
# still here. Half a fix that reads like a whole one is its own hazard.
#
# WHAT WAS DONE ABOUT IT:
#
#   * The `install` and `rm -f` rules are DELETED, not narrowed. No Cmnd_Alias
#     pattern can fix them in place — any surviving `*` re-absorbs `-t`/`-m`/
#     `-o`/`-d`, and a wildcard argument spec cannot express "exactly one
#     argument". Unit installation now happens in `ops/src/units.ts`: the
#     executor is `User=root`, so it writes the file itself, with a validated
#     unit NAME, content read from a fixed staging path, and a destination,
#     mode and owner it constructs. That is the same argument this file has
#     always made for run-container.sh and snapshot.sh, applied to the one
#     capability that had been left in sudo.
#   * The `journalctl` rule is DELETED. Journal reading is `systemd-journal`
#     group membership, which needs no sudo and cannot vacuum anything.
#     `ops/src/agent-user.ts` warns on every task until the membership exists.
#   * `docker inspect *` is replaced by enumerated `--format` invocations that
#     cannot print an environment.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-11, AND WHY THIS FILE IS NOW WORTH READING
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Until 2026-08-11 this file granted its rules to `npurcell` — the operator —
# and the version of it written on 2026-08-10 said so plainly in a paragraph
# headed "be clear about what this does and does not buy":
#
#     It does NOT contain the host agent. It cannot. npurcell is in the
#     `docker` group on this host — SETUP.md says so in the line that adds it,
#     "docker group is effectively root on the host" — and `docker run -v
#     /:/host` is root with no sudo involved at all.
#
# That was honest and it was also fatal to the whole exercise. A carefully
# scoped command alias granted to a member of the docker group is not a rule;
# it is a description of the polite route. Every wildcard below was therefore
# free — `systemctl restart *` could take out sshd, but so could
# `docker run -v /:/host` — and nobody had to think about it.
#
# The host agent now runs as its own unprivileged system account, by default
# `clawcius-ops`, which is deliberately NOT in `docker`, `sudo`, `wheel`,
# `adm`, `disk` or any other root-equivalent group. `ops/src/agent-user.ts`
# REFUSES TO START A SESSION as an account that is, checked against the live
# /etc/group before every single task rather than once at boot — because the
# `usermod -aG docker clawcius-ops` this is guarding against is something
# somebody types on a running host to make a task work.
#
# So for the first time the contents of this file are the actual bound on what
# the session can do, and every wildcard in it had to go.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT THIS FILE IS, AND — STILL — WHAT IT IS NOT
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# It IS:
#
#   1. **The bound on the easy path, and the easy path is the audited one.**
#      Every command the session runs is written into
#      /var/lib/clawcius-ops/journal.jsonl, in full, before its result is
#      known. Granting `ALL` would make "get a root shell" one command;
#      granting these makes the obvious way to do each job the way that reads
#      clearly in the log.
#   2. **A blast-radius limit inside this project's own namespace.** No sshd,
#      no systemd-journald, no nftables/ufw, no docker.service, no `docker
#      run`, no apt. A task that goes wrong in the way tasks go wrong — a typo,
#      a confused model, a badly worded request — cannot take the host's
#      network or its login off the air.
#   3. **A diff.** Widening it is a change somebody reviews, which is the whole
#      point of it living in the repository rather than being typed into visudo
#      at 3am.
#
# It is NOT a boundary against a determined adversary, and here is the precise
# reason, stated rather than left to be discovered. THIS PARAGRAPH WAS REWRITTEN
# ON 2026-08-12, because the version it replaces said "root in two audited steps"
# while the `install` rule above it made root ONE step, and an honest-sounding
# admission of a bigger problem than the file actually had is how the smaller
# true one stopped being looked for:
#
#   **Deploying a unit file plus restarting a named unit is root, in three
#   audited steps.** The steps are: ask the executor to install a unit (see
#   `ops/src/units.ts`), `systemctl daemon-reload`, `systemctl restart
#   clawcius.service`. The last two are granted below. The first is no longer a
#   sudo rule at all — but the CONTENT of the unit is still written by the
#   session, so a unit whose ExecStart is anything, reloaded and restarted, is
#   still root. There is no way to remove that without also removing the ability
#   to deploy this project's own units, which is the capability the whole ops
#   mechanism exists to provide.
#
#   What it costs the adversary is that all three steps are in the journal, in
#   full, fsynced before they ran, and they look like exactly what they are —
#   the install as its own `"kind":"unit"` entry naming the destination, the
#   other two as audited commands. That is the trade, and it is the same trade
#   the audit log makes everywhere else in this design.
#
#   What is NEW since 2026-08-12 is what this is no longer: it is not a way to
#   write an arbitrary file to an arbitrary path with an arbitrary mode, and it
#   is not a way to read a root-only file out through /etc/systemd/system. It
#   reaches this project's own units, by name, and nothing else. `oj.service`
#   is in reach; `ssh.service`, `/etc/sudoers.d/anything` and
#   `clawcius-ops.service` are not.
#
# One more, and it is new since the account changed: **any sudo rule naming a
# path inside the checkout would be equivalent to `sudo ALL`.** The agent can
# write /home/npurcell/clawcius (that is the point of the shared group in
# MIGRATION.md § 2), so `sudo /home/npurcell/clawcius/docker/run-container.sh`
# would be "run this file I can edit, as root". That is why there is no rule
# for run-container.sh or snapshot.sh here even though the agent obviously
# needs containers recreated: **the executor runs those two scripts itself, as
# root, with an argv it builds** — see ops/src/executor.ts. Never add a sudo
# rule pointing into a tree this account can write.
#
# And the corollary that took until 2026-08-12 to be acted on: **when a
# capability cannot be expressed safely as a sudo rule, it belongs in the
# executor, not in a cleverer pattern.** The executor is root already. Anything
# it does has a destination built in code and an argv it constructed, which is
# a class of correctness that `fnmatch` over a flattened string cannot reach.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# INSTALLING IT
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root \
#     /home/npurcell/clawcius/ops/clawcius-sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/clawcius
#
# CHECK IT PARSES BEFORE YOU RELY ON IT. A syntax error in one file under
# /etc/sudoers.d breaks sudo for everybody, including the shell you would use
# to fix it:
#
#   sudo visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/clawcius
#
# Do that from a SECOND shell that already holds root. See MIGRATION.md § 3.
#
# NOT YET VERIFIED WITH visudo -c. Still true on 2026-08-12, and re-checked
# rather than copied forward: the machine this was edited on has no `sudo` and
# no `visudo` binary at all (`apt-get download sudo` cannot even find the
# package), so this file has never been parsed by the thing that will parse it.
#
# Two syntax details to look at first if it does not parse, both introduced on
# 2026-08-12 with the `docker inspect` enumeration:
#
#   * the `{{.State.Status}}` format strings. Braces are ordinary characters to
#     sudoers' lexer, and there is no comma inside them, but this is the first
#     time this file has used any punctuation of that shape.
#   * the `\:` in `clawcius-agent\:latest`. A colon is a sudoers metacharacter
#     and has to be escaped in a command argument — the same escape the
#     `chown npurcell\:npurcell` lines below have carried since 2026-08-11.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TWO THINGS ABOUT SUDO'S MATCHING THAT DECIDE THE SHAPE OF THIS FILE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# 1. **Unit names must be written in full, with the suffix.** `systemctl
#    restart clawcius` and `systemctl restart clawcius.service` do the same
#    thing to systemd and are different strings to sudo, and only the second
#    one is listed below. If a command here is refused with "a password is
#    required", the shorthand is the first thing to check. Both spellings are
#    NOT listed, deliberately: the value of this file is that it can be read in
#    one sitting, and doubling it to absorb an abbreviation would cost more
#    than the refusal does.
#
# 2. **`*` matches everything, including `/` and spaces.** sudo joins the
#    arguments into one string and fnmatch()es it without FNM_PATHNAME. So the
#    old `chown … /var/lib/clawcius*` rule matched
#    `/var/lib/clawcius/../../etc`, and the old `systemctl restart *` matched
#    literally any unit. That was documented and shrugged off in the 2026-08-10
#    version, on the grounds that the docker group made it moot. It is not moot
#    any more, so the paths below are ENUMERATED rather than globbed.
#
#    The 2026-08-11 version then said: "where a `*` survives it is either in a
#    read-only command or in a position where what it matches cannot escape a
#    fixed prefix AND a fixed suffix." The second half of that sentence is
#    WRONG, and it is the sentence the 2026-08-12 audit walked straight through.
#    A fixed prefix and a fixed suffix bound the FIRST and LAST token of the
#    flattened argument string and nothing in between — not the number of
#    arguments, not their content, not whether they are flags. `rm -f
#    /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service` has a fixed prefix and a fixed
#    suffix and deletes /var/lib/clawcius-ops/journal.jsonl.
#
#    THE RULE THIS FILE NOW FOLLOWS, and it is narrower on purpose:
#
#      A `*` may appear ONLY after a pinned, read-only SUBCOMMAND, where the
#      worst thing the wildcard can absorb is more read-only flags for that
#      same subcommand.
#
#    That is why `systemctl status *` and `docker ps *` are still here: the verb
#    is the first token, getopt permutation cannot change which verb ran, and
#    every flag those verbs accept only prints things. It is why `journalctl *`
#    is NOT here any more (its flags include `--vacuum-time` and `--rotate`,
#    which destroy), why `docker inspect *` is not (`--format` prints
#    `Config.Env`), and why nothing that WRITES takes a `*` in any position at
#    all. If you are about to add a `*`, the question is not "can this path
#    escape" — it is "what else does this program do when handed more
#    arguments".
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ADDING SOMETHING
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A new unit, a new container or a new state directory means one more line
# here, a `visudo -c`, and a re-install. That cost is deliberate. The 2026-08-10
# version removed the unit list precisely because "the evening was spent
# standing up NEW units, which by definition are not in any list" — and that
# argument is still right about *installing* a unit (which is still possible
# without editing this file at all, since 2026-08-12, because it is done by the
# executor against a name pattern rather than by a rule against a path) and
# wrong about *starting* one (which is running new code as root, forever, and
# is the single step most worth a human's attention).

# ── Who ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match `hostAgent.user` in ops-config.yaml. If the two ever disagree the
# symptom is every privileged command in a task failing with a password prompt
# that nothing can answer, which reads like a broken sudoers file rather than
# like a mismatched name.
User_Alias CLAWCIUS_AGENT = clawcius-ops

# ── Environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `env_reset` is sudo's default and is restated because the next line depends
# on it. The `env_delete` additions are not decoration:
#
# `sudo journalctl` will page its output, and a pager is a shell. `less`
# reaches a root shell with `!sh` in one keystroke, and `journalctl` honours
# $SYSTEMD_PAGER and $PAGER to decide which pager to run. In practice the
# session is not a tty and journalctl does not page — but "in practice" and "is
# not a tty" are exactly the phrases that precede an incident in this
# repository, and deleting the variables costs nothing.
Defaults:CLAWCIUS_AGENT env_reset
Defaults:CLAWCIUS_AGENT env_delete += "PAGER SYSTEMD_PAGER SYSTEMD_LESS LESS LESSOPEN LESSCLOSE LESSSECURE EDITOR VISUAL SUDO_EDITOR"
# There is no terminal. Without this, a rule that ever needed a password would
# hang until the task timeout instead of failing in a second with a readable
# message. Every rule here is NOPASSWD, so this is belt and braces.
Defaults:CLAWCIUS_AGENT !requiretty
# CONSIDERED AND REJECTED: `log_input, log_output, iolog_dir=…`. sudo's I/O
# logging would give a second, independent record of the privileged subset that
# this account cannot rewrite — genuinely attractive, since the executor's own
# journal is the only record today. It is not enabled because `sudo journalctl
# -u clawcius -n 5000` would then be written to disk twice, in full, on a host
# whose known gaps already include ~2 GB snapshot images, and a full disk turns
# every one of this daemon's recovery paths into a failure at once. sudo's
# ordinary syslog line (who ran what, as whom) is on by default and is the
# cheap 90% of the value. Revisit if the journal is ever suspected of being
# tampered with.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# systemd — units this project owns, by name
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The mutating verbs. Every unit in `systemd/` is here, plus the two OJ units
# that do not exist yet (naming a unit that is not installed grants nothing and
# saves an edit on the day it is).
#
# ── clawcius-ops.service is DELIBERATELY ABSENT ───────────────────────────
#
# It is the process that starts the host agent. Restarting it sends SIGTERM to
# the session's parent mid-task: the task dies, systemd brings the daemon back
# with an empty queue, the operation is simply gone — no failure, no journal
# entry saying it did not finish — and the audit ends mid-sentence with nothing
# saying why. Worse, the armed rollback deadlines are re-read from disk on
# boot, so a restart during a task's deadline window is a recovery window
# silently restarted.
#
# Until 2026-08-11 this was prevented by a sentence in the standing prompt
# ("You cannot restart clawcius-ops.service. Ask the operator."). An
# instruction can be talked out of; a missing sudoers line cannot. It is now
# both: the prompt still explains WHY, so the refusal the session hits is
# understood rather than routed around.
#
# Reading its status and its journal IS granted, below, because a session
# debugging its own supervisor is useful and cannot break it.
#
# ── docker.service is DELIBERATELY ABSENT ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The 2026-08-10 version noted that removing the unit list had made
# docker.service reachable, and called that "part of the trade". Restarting
# docker takes every container on this host down at once — both agents, mid
# conversation — and there is no task for which that is the smallest change
# that fixes the thing. It is back out of reach.
#
# sshd/ssh, systemd-journald, nftables, ufw, systemd-resolved and the rest of
# the base system are absent for the obvious reason: a confused task that stops
# one of them ends the operator's ability to reach the box, and no ops task has
# ever needed to.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_RESTART = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart oj-container.service

Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_START = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start oj-container.service

Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_STOP = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop oj-container.service

# enable/disable change what happens at the NEXT boot, which is a different and
# slower-acting kind of damage than a restart — a task that disables
# clawcius.service leaves everything working until a reboot nobody connects to
# it. Same list, deliberately, so there is one set of units to reason about.
# reset-failed is here rather than with the read-only verbs because it clears
# state systemd is using to hold a unit down.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_ENABLE = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable oj-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable oj-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed oj-container.service

# daemon-reload takes no unit and changes nothing by itself; it is what makes
# an installed unit file visible. Bare, with no arguments, so it cannot absorb
# one.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_DAEMON_RELOAD = /usr/bin/systemctl daemon-reload

# ── Read-only systemctl, any unit, on purpose ─────────────────────────────
#
# Answering "what is the state of this box" cannot break it, and the whole
# reason this service exists is that the operator was reading unit states out
# loud to an agent that could not see them. Restricting these to the project's
# own units would mean an agent debugging "clawcius.service will not start"
# could not look at the unit it depends on.
#
# The one honest caveat: `systemctl show` prints a unit's inline `Environment=`
# values. None of this project's units carry a secret inline — they use
# EnvironmentFile, and `show` prints only the PATH of those — but a third-party
# unit on this host that inlined a credential would be readable here. Noted
# rather than papered over; unit files under /etc/systemd/system are
# world-readable anyway, so this grants nothing `cat` does not.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_READ = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl status *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-active *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-enabled *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-failed *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl show *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl cat *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl list-units *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl list-timers *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl list-unit-files *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl --failed, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl ""

# The `""` on the bare entry above is not decoration. A command listed in
# sudoers with NO argument spec permits that command with ANY arguments — so
# a bare `/usr/bin/systemctl` line grants `systemctl restart sshd.service`,
# `systemctl stop` anything, and every other invocation the careful list above
# it spells out one unit at a time. `""` means "no arguments", which is what
# was intended: bare `systemctl` lists units.
#
# Found on 2026-08-12 by the operator running the step-3 refusal check:
# `sudo -u clawcius-ops sudo -n /usr/bin/systemctl restart sshd.service`
# returned "Unit sshd.service not found" rather than a refusal — sudo had
# permitted it and systemctl merely had nothing to restart. The enumeration
# had been decorative from the moment it was written.

# `systemctl edit` and `set-property` are NOT here and never should be: `edit`
# opens $EDITOR as root, which is a root shell with extra steps, and the
# env_delete line above exists partly so that a future mistake in this file
# does not immediately become one.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# journalctl — the thing that started all of this, and it is NOT here any more
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The specific pain point is unchanged and it is the reason this whole service
# exists: the operator was reading `journalctl -u …` output out loud to an agent
# that could not see it. What changed on 2026-08-12 is HOW the agent reads it.
#
# There is no `journalctl` rule here. There was one, it was
#
#     Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_LOGS = /usr/bin/journalctl "", /usr/bin/journalctl *
#
# and the comment above it said "Read-only, any unit". That was false. `*`
# carries every mutating option the binary has:
#
#     sudo journalctl --rotate --vacuum-time=1s     # the log history, gone
#     sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=1B              # same, by another name
#     sudo journalctl --flush --relinquish-var      # move where it is written
#
# An agent — or anyone who has taken one over — could erase the host's log
# history as root, in one command, from an alias called LOGS. Combined with the
# `rm -f` rule that reached journal.jsonl, that was both halves of the audit
# trail, and the design's stated fallback for everything else is "it is not a
# boundary, but it is all in the log".
#
# It could not be narrowed in place. journalctl's getopt permutes, so a `*`
# anywhere in the argument spec re-admits `--vacuum-time`; and the reading forms
# an agent actually uses (`-u X -n 200 --since '1 hour ago'`) cannot be
# enumerated, because the unit name and the count are the variables.
#
# THE REPLACEMENT: `clawcius-ops` goes in the `systemd-journal` group.
#
#     sudo usermod -aG systemd-journal clawcius-ops
#     sudo systemctl restart clawcius-ops
#
# Then plain `journalctl -u clawcius.service -n 200` works with no sudo at all,
# and `--vacuum-*`/`--rotate` fail because they need to WRITE the journal, which
# the group does not grant. This is strictly better than any rule that could
# have been written here: the capability is read-only by construction rather than
# by a pattern somebody has to keep correct.
#
# Note what it is NOT: it is not `adm`. `adm` also reads /var/log/auth.log,
# which is where sudo records what this account did, and it stays on the
# refused-groups list in ops/src/agent-user.ts. `systemd-journal` reads the
# journal and nothing else.
#
# If the membership is missing, every task carries a warning in the journal
# naming the one command that fixes it (`agentWarnings`), and the session's
# standing prompt tells it to report the failure rather than look for a way
# round. That is deliberate: making it a REFUSAL would mean a missing usermod
# takes the whole ops mechanism offline, and the way that gets fixed at 3am is
# by putting this rule back.
#
# The env_delete line above stays. It was written for the pager escape in
# `sudo journalctl`, and now guards `sudo systemctl status` — which pages too.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# docker — named containers, read-mostly, and NOT `docker run`
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# This section did not exist before 2026-08-11. It could not: the previous
# grantee reached docker through group membership, so a sudoers rule "would add
# nothing except the false impression that docker access is being controlled
# here". The agent account is not in the docker group, so these rules are now
# the entire docker capability the session has, and every line is a decision.
#
# NOT GRANTED, and each for its own reason:
#
#   * `docker run` / `docker create` — this is THE hole. `docker run -v /:/host
#     alpine chroot /host sh` is root, immediately, with no audit inside it.
#     Nothing the host agent legitimately does needs it: the two containers on
#     this host are created by docker/run-container.sh, which the EXECUTOR runs
#     as root with an argv it builds itself.
#   * `docker exec` — `docker exec -u root clawcius-agent …` is root inside a
#     crew's container, holding its Claude credential, its Discord token and
#     every live session's transcript. It is also a shell as the uid that owns
#     that instance's whole state directory.
#   * `docker cp` — copies as root, to and from arbitrary host paths. It is
#     `sudo cp` wearing a container.
#   * `docker build` / `load` / `import` / `commit` / `save` — a build runs a
#     Dockerfile as root, and the rest move image layers around.
#   * `docker system prune`, `volume rm`, `network rm` — destructive, and since
#     2026-08-16 nothing takes a snapshot before a task, so a pruned image is
#     one a person was relying on.
#
# Most read-only verbs take a `*` because their arguments are flags and format
# strings, the verb itself is pinned as the first token, and every flag those
# verbs accept only prints things. `ps`, `images`, `stats --no-stream` and
# `logs` are in that class.
#
# ── `inspect` is NOT in that class, and used to be treated as if it were ──
#
# Until 2026-08-12 this alias contained `docker inspect *`, `docker container
# inspect *` and `docker image inspect *`, under a heading that says "read-only"
# and a sentence saying "there is nothing on the far side of them to protect".
# There is:
#
#     sudo docker inspect clawcius-agent hamachi-agent oj-agent
#
# prints `Config.Env` in full — the API keys and tokens the SIBLING agent
# containers run with — plus their whole bind-mount table. This file discloses
# the far smaller `systemctl show` leak two sections up and argues it "grants
# nothing `cat` does not"; for `inspect` that argument is simply false. Container
# environment is not world-readable, and this account is deliberately not in the
# docker group, so the rule WAS the entire capability. It also quietly undid the
# `docker exec` denial below, whose stated purpose is keeping one instance out of
# another's provenance: the credentials read here are what let you impersonate
# that instance somewhere else.
#
# So `inspect` is enumerated by container AND by format. The formats are chosen
# to answer "is it up, did it die, and when" and nothing else. `--format` is
# spelled out in full because `-f` is a different string to sudo — same rule as
# unit names, and the same deliberate refusal to double the file to absorb an
# abbreviation. There is no `*` anywhere on these lines: a trailing one would
# re-admit a second `--format`, and docker takes the last.
#
# If a format that is genuinely needed is missing, that is one more line here
# and a `visudo -c`. Anything that would print `.Config.Env`, `.Config.Cmd`,
# `.Mounts` or the whole object does not go in — the executor's briefing already
# tells the session every container's state, and "read another agent's
# credentials" is not a debugging step.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_READ = \
    /usr/bin/docker ps, \
    /usr/bin/docker ps *, \
    /usr/bin/docker images, \
    /usr/bin/docker images *, \
    /usr/bin/docker version, \
    /usr/bin/docker info, \
    /usr/bin/docker stats --no-stream, \
    /usr/bin/docker stats --no-stream *, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.Status}} clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.Status}} hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.Status}} oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.Running}} clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.Running}} hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.Running}} oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.ExitCode}} clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.ExitCode}} hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.ExitCode}} oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.OOMKilled}} clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.OOMKilled}} hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.OOMKilled}} oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.StartedAt}} clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.StartedAt}} hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.State.StartedAt}} oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.RestartCount}} clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.RestartCount}} hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect --format {{.RestartCount}} oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker image inspect --format {{.Id}} clawcius-agent\:latest, \
    /usr/bin/docker image inspect --format {{.Id}} hamachi-agent\:latest, \
    /usr/bin/docker image inspect --format {{.Id}} oj-agent\:latest, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs * clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs * hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs * oj-agent

# Restarting a container kills whatever turn it was in the middle of, which is
# why the executor waits for an idle turn before anything destructive. The
# session gets these because "the agent container is wedged" is a real thing a
# task is asked to fix, and because the deadline machinery notices a container
# name in the audit and arms a check-in for it.
#
# There is no `docker rm`. Removing an agent container discards its writable
# layer, and the way to get one back is the executor's rollback path, not a
# task's.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_LIFECYCLE = \
    /usr/bin/docker restart clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker restart hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker restart oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker stop clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker stop hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker stop oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker start clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker start hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker start oj-agent

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# State directories — enumerated, because `*` matches `..`
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The 2026-08-10 version scoped these by path glob (`/var/lib/clawcius*`) and
# documented, honestly, that `chown … /var/lib/clawcius/../../etc` matches —
# concluding that "on a host where npurcell is already in the docker group that
# changes nothing about what is reachable". That conclusion was correct and its
# premise is gone, so the glob had to go with it. These are exact paths.
#
# Most of this is repair work rather than routine: the executor creates and
# chowns the host agent's working directory at boot (`ensureDirOwnedBy`, in
# ops/src/dirs.ts), and `docker/run-container.sh` creates `<stateDir>/run` as
# npurcell before it starts a container. These rules are for the case where that
# went wrong, and for standing up a new instance.
#
# Note the absence of /home/npurcell: the agent shares that tree through a
# group, not through sudo, and a `sudo chown` inside it is how the 2026-08-09
# root-owned-node_modules incident got "fixed" twice. Note also the absence of
# /var/lib/clawcius-ops: it holds journal.jsonl, the circuit breaker and the
# armed deadlines.
#
# ── "No rule here reaches it" — true since 2026-08-12, false before ──────
#
# The 2026-08-11 version of this paragraph said "No rule here reaches it, and
# for the first time that is a statement about what the session can do rather
# than a statement about what it would have to bother doing." It was checking
# the wrong section. The rules in THIS block are exact paths and none of them
# reached it, but `rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service`, in the unit
# block below, did:
#
#     sudo rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawciusA.service \
#                /var/lib/clawcius-ops/journal.jsonl \
#                /var/lib/clawcius-ops/breaker.json \
#                /etc/systemd/system/clawciusB.service
#
# matched, because `*` matches spaces and only the first and last token were
# anchored. The audit log, the breaker and every armed deadline were one command
# away, and the sentence at the bottom of this file listing them as things that
# appear "in no rule, by any path" was the reassurance that stopped anybody
# checking. That rule is gone. The claim is now true, and it is true because the
# grep is empty rather than because of an argument: no rule below names
# /var/lib/clawcius-ops, and no rule below takes a `*` in a writing position at
# all, so there is no string that reaches it.
# The six /run/ops and six /run/wake entries that used to be here went on
# 2026-08-16 with the spools they created. Nothing reads or writes either
# directory now, and a grant nobody uses is a grant nobody re-reads either — the
# whole argument of this file is that the list is short enough to check.
#
# The state directory and its `run/` remain, because `docker/run-container.sh`
# still bind-mounts `<stateDir>/run` and a first start on a fresh instance still
# has to create it.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_STATE_MKDIR = \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/clawcius, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/clawcius/run, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/hamachi, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/hamachi/run, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/oj, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/oj/run

# The uid:gid pairs are spelled out rather than matched with a character class,
# because `[a-z]*\:[a-z]*` accepts `root:root` and the whole question this rule
# answers is "who ends up owning the directory the container writes". npurcell
# is the answer for the instance state directories, because that is the uid the
# containers run as (the Dockerfile builds the agent user with AGENT_UID=1000
# to match).
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_STATE_CHOWN = \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/clawcius/run, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/hamachi/run, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/oj/run, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/clawcius/run, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/hamachi/run, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/oj/run

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Unit files — NOT HERE ANY MORE, and this section is the reason why
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# There is no `install` rule and no `rm` rule in this file. Until 2026-08-12
# there was a `Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNITS` containing eight lines shaped like
#
#     /usr/bin/install -m 0644 -o root -g root * /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service
#     /usr/bin/rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service
#
# above a paragraph which said, in this order: that `install` was chosen over
# `cp` "because it takes the mode and ownership as arguments, so the rule can
# pin them: a unit file arrives 0644 root:root or it does not arrive"; that the
# destination was namespaced; that the trailing `*` could not climb out "because
# there is no `..` that produces a path still ending in a project-prefixed unit
# name"; and then — to its credit — "which is a sentence worth distrusting".
#
# Every one of those claims was false, and the last one was right to distrust
# itself. The audit strings are in the header at the top of this file. The
# summary is that the middle `*` absorbed further `install` flags, which GNU
# install applies last-wins, so `-t /etc/sudoers.d` redirected the write, `-m
# 4755` unpinned the mode, an absolute source read any root-only file, `rm -f`
# with two project-prefixed names around it deleted anything in between, and
# `clawcius/../ssh.service` matched the "namespaced" destination.
#
# ── Why it was deleted rather than narrowed ─────────────────────────────
#
# Because it cannot be narrowed. Any surviving `*` re-absorbs `-t`/`-m`/`-o`/
# `-d`; a wildcard argument spec cannot express "exactly one argument"; and
# enumerating destinations would still leave the SOURCE free, which is the leak
# that copies /root/.ssh/id_ed25519 into a world-readable path. The only rule
# shape that would work is one pointing at a root-owned wrapper OUTSIDE the
# checkout — and at that point the wrapper is doing, in shell, what the executor
# can do in code with an argv it built and a journal entry it wrote.
#
# ── Where the capability went ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `ops/src/units.ts`. clawcius-ops.service is `User=root`, so the executor
# installs and removes units ITSELF — which is exactly the argument this file
# already makes for run-container.sh and snapshot.sh a hundred lines up. The
# session stages the unit's content in its own working directory and files a
# two-field request; the executor validates the NAME against
# `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*\.(service|timer)$` plus explicit refusals for separators,
# `..`, whitespace and control bytes, requires one of this project's prefixes,
# refuses clawcius-ops.service outright, reads the content through an O_NOFOLLOW
# descriptor so a staged symlink cannot be published, and writes to a path IT
# computes with a mode and owner it sets on the descriptor.
#
# So the claim the old comment made is finally true, and it is true for a
# different reason: a unit file arrives 0644 root:root or it does not arrive —
# because `fchmod`/`fchown` ran on the file that was written, not because a
# pattern in a text file asked an `install` invocation to behave.
#
# Every install and every refusal is a `"kind":"unit"` entry in journal.jsonl
# naming the destination, so `grep '"kind":"unit"'` is the complete list of what
# has been written into /etc/systemd/system by this machinery.
#
# What is unchanged: the session still authors the CONTENT, and installing a
# unit then restarting it is still root. See the paragraph at the top. What is
# gone is everything else that rule turned out to grant.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# What is deliberately NOT here
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# * Any blanket interpreter: no `sudo sh`, `bash`, `python3`, `perl`, `env`,
#   `tee`, `cp`, `dd`, `mv`, `ln`, `vi`, `find`, `awk`, `xargs`. Those are the
#   classic one-line escalations, and each would also be a one-line way to
#   rewrite the audit log that stands where the verb allowlist used to.
# * `install` and `rm`, in any form, at any path. Added to this list on
#   2026-08-12 with the rest of the audit: they belong in the same sentence as
#   `cp` and `mv` above and were treated as a special case for two days because
#   the flags on them looked like a constraint. `install` is `cp` that can also
#   set the mode and the owner; `rm` with a wildcard argument spec cannot be
#   made to mean one file. Unit installation is in ops/src/units.ts.
# * `journalctl`. Removed on 2026-08-12. `--vacuum-time`, `--vacuum-size`,
#   `--rotate`, `--flush` and `--relinquish-var` destroy or move the host's log
#   history, so "read the logs" is the `systemd-journal` group, not a sudo rule.
#   See the section above.
# * apt / npm / pip as root. Installing packages system-wide has a blast radius
#   beyond this host's lifetime and is one of the very few things worth waiting
#   for a human on. A task that needs a system package should say so in its
#   report and stop.
# * visudo, usermod, useradd, gpasswd, groupadd, passwd, chsh, su. Granting the
#   ability to grant is the end of the exercise — and `usermod -aG docker
#   clawcius-ops` is now, specifically and by name, the thing this account must
#   not be able to do to itself.
# * setfacl / chattr. Both are ways to reach the operator's files past the mode
#   bits that ops/src/agent-user.ts checks.
# * mount / umount. A bind mount of /home somewhere the mode-bit check does not
#   look would defeat the secret-readability assertion silently.
# * /var/lib/clawcius-ops, in any rule, by any path. This line was here on
#   2026-08-11 and was NOT true then: `rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service`
#   reached it, because `*` matches spaces and only the first and last token of
#   the flattened argument string were anchored. It is true now, and the way to
#   keep it true is the rule two sections up — no `*` in a writing position, in
#   any rule, ever. A line in this section is a claim about the whole file, so
#   check the whole file before adding one.
# * docker.service, sshd, systemd-journald, the firewall, and everything else
#   in the base system. See the systemd section.

# Two aliases fewer than on 2026-08-11: CLAWCIUS_LOGS and CLAWCIUS_UNITS are
# both gone, and the capabilities they carried moved to the systemd-journal
# group and to ops/src/units.ts respectively. Nothing granted below writes a
# file: every remaining rule either changes the state of a NAMED unit or
# container, prints something, or creates/chowns one of twelve enumerated
# directories.
CLAWCIUS_AGENT ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: CLAWCIUS_UNIT_RESTART, CLAWCIUS_UNIT_START, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_UNIT_STOP, CLAWCIUS_UNIT_ENABLE, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_DAEMON_RELOAD, CLAWCIUS_UNIT_READ, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_READ, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_LIFECYCLE, CLAWCIUS_STATE_MKDIR, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_STATE_CHOWN
